      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of 1/7, we will perform the long division of 1 by 7.\n\nHere's the breakdown of the long division process:\n\n1. Divide 1 by 7. The quotient is 0, and the remainder is 1 (since 7 goes into 1 zero times).\n\n2. Bring down a decimal point and a 0 after the remainder, making it 10.\n\n3. Now, divide 10 by 7. The quotient is 1, and the remainder is 3 (since 7 goes into 10 once).\n\n4. Bring down another 0 after the remainder, making it 30.\n\n5. Divide 30 by 7. The quotient is 4, and the remainder is 2 (since 7 goes into 30 four times).\n\n6. Bring down another 0 after the remainder, making it 20.\n\n7. Divide 20 by 7. The quotient is 2, and the remainder is 6 (since 7 goes into 20 twice).\n\n8. Bring down another 0 after the remainder, making it 60.\n\n9. Divide 60 by 7. The quotient is 8, and the remainder is 4 (since 7 goes into 60 eight times).\n\n10. Bring down another 0 after the remainder, making it 40.\n\n11. Divide 40 by 7. The quotient is 5, and the remainder is 5 (since 7 goes into 40 five times).\n\nWe can notice a pattern here. The digits after the decimal point start repeating every 6 digits: 285301. This makes a cycle of length 6.\n\nTo find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we can determine the position of the 100th digit within this cycle. We do this by finding the remainder when 99 (since we start counting from 1 for the first digit) is divided by 6.\n\n99 ÷ 6 = 16 with a remainder of 3.\n\nThis means the 100th digit is the same as the 3rd digit in the cycle. The 3rd digit in the cycle is 5. So, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of 1/7 is \\boxed{5}.",
