      "text": "To find the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we start by performing the division:\n\n\\[ 1 \\div 7 = 0.142857142857\\ldots \\]\n\nThis repetition of digits \\( 142857 \\) is known as the repeating decimal sequence for \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\). \n\nTo determine the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to identify the place value of the 100th digit within the repeating sequence \\( 142857 \\).\n\nFirst, calculate the length of the repeating cycle:\n\n\\[ 142857 \\]\n\nThe length of this cycle is 6.\n\nNext, determine the position of the 100th digit within this repeating cycle by computing the remainder of \\( 100 \\) modulo 6:\n\n\\[ 100 \\mod 6 = 4 \\]\n\nThis means the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \\( 142857 \\) is the 100th digit after the decimal point.\n\nWe now look at the first 4 digits of the sequence \\( 142857 \\):\n\n\\[ 142857 \\]\n\nThe 100th digit in this list is the digit at position \\( 4 \\):\n\n\\[ [1, 4, 2, 8, 5, 7]_{\\text{position } 4} \\]\n\nIdentifying the specific digit at position \\( 4 \\):\n\n\\[ 8 \\]\n\nThus, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{8} \\]",
